  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nSfc high pressure remains centered across southern MN/northern IA \nthis afternoon while an expansive area of low pressure sits over the \nDakotas/MT/WY border region. Southerly flow has returned a warm and \nmore moist air mass into our region today with afternoon dew point \ntemperatures in the upper 50s to mid 60s. An ACCAS field continues \nto drift northeast across parts of central and northeast SD this \nafternoon but so far it remains dry underneath this feature. There \nwere some hints that this warm advection regime setting up would \ntouch off an isolated shower or storm this afternoon across \nnortheast SD, particularly the Big Sioux Valley area. It remains a \nminor mention in the forecast but confidence it low as most CAMs are \nnot showing anything occurring.\n\nConditions more favorable for convection will become more evident \nlater tonight into early Sunday as the sfc low drifts southeast and \nmeets up with a southward sagging frontal boundary coming out of ND \ninto northern SD. Broad northwest flow aloft will remain persistent \nthat could include passage of some mid level energy. A nocturnal low \nlevel jet will interact with moderate to strong CAPE and steep low-\nmid level lapse rates along with strong deep layer shear to kick off \nthunderstorm activity. CAMs seem to focus initiation ND tonight and \nthen track the activity south and east through our CWA. This could \noccur as early as mid to late evening but our better window for \nsevere weather looks to be after midnight. Given the favorable \nparameters, very large hail up to 2 inches will be the main threat \nalong with damaging wind gusts of 60 mph. For now, a Marginal Risk \n(1 of 5) for severe weather remains posted for most of the CWA. \nWhatever comes of the overnight convection, guidance progs it to \nshift out of our area by mid morning leaving us with a mainly dry, \nwarm and humid day with highs expected to reach the low to mid 80s.\n\nWe'll repeat the same setup again by Sunday night into Monday. \nNorthwest flow aloft will persist as sfc winds veer east to \nsoutheast heading into Monday. Warm advection returning should help \nrecover the atmosphere back into a favorable environment for \nconvection by the end of the day through Sunday night. Moderate to \nstrong instability and strong deep layer shear will help contribute \nto the development of convection Sunday evening into early Monday \nmorning. A nocturnal low level jet across western/central SD will \nhelp to maintain activity through the overnight. Initial threats \nwill be large hail perhaps up to golfball size. However, with time a \ndamaging wind threat could become the main threat if an MCS is able \nto develop and track southeast across parts of the forecast area. A \nMarginal Risk (1 of 5) for severe weather covers most of the area \nwith a Slight Risk (2 of 5) for severe weather has been posted for \nparts of central/northeast SD.\n\nAn active pattern is expected to spill over into the early portion \nof the new week. More convection will be possible by late Monday \ninto early Tuesday as the set up remains mostly unchanged with a \ndisturbance working into the western Dakotas. Some storms could be \nstrong to severe across mainly western and central SD Monday night. \nWildfire smoke will remain prevalent across the region for the next \ncouple days. HRRR vertically integrated smoke product shows moderate \nto high concentrations of smoke in the upper layers of the atmosphere \nwill persist into Monday. Some good news on this front is that it \nappear most of this smoke will stay aloft and not affect sfc \nconditions.",
